(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-7-pleural-disorders-vignettes-version-2"]={"title":"Pleural Disorders Vignettes 2 — CMS I Exam 7","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 7","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 66-year-old man develops a right pleural effusion 5 days after thoracic surgery. Thoracentesis returns milky white fluid. Which pleural fluid result confirms the suspected diagnosis?","choices":["Purulent material","Malignant cells","Hematocrit above half of blood's","Elevated triglycerides"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Chylothorax is thoracic duct disruption that lets lipid from chyle collect in the pleural space, and trauma such as thoracic surgery is its most common cause. Thoracentesis shows milky fluid with elevated triglycerides. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 74, 98, 99, 104, 105, 108"},{"q":"A 64-year-old woman who was treated for breast cancer 8 years ago has a new right pleural effusion with no other clinical cause, and diagnostic thoracentesis is performed. Which pleural fluid study is sent specifically to detect a malignant effusion?","choices":["Cytology","Gram stain","Microbiological culture","Triglyceride level"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Pleural fluid is sent for protein, lactate dehydrogenase, white cell count with differential, Gram stain, cytology and microbiological culture, and cytology is the study that detects malignant effusions. Malignant effusions can occur years after a cancer diagnosis, and breast carcinoma is among their leading causes. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 84, 96, 99, 102, 105"},{"q":"A 72-year-old man with severe chronic obstructive pulmonary disease develops a spontaneous pneumothorax. His daughter, who had a pneumothorax at age 24 with no lung disease, asks how his episode is likely to differ from hers. Which statement is accurate?","choices":["Less urgent; home observation","More severe; less reserve","Milder; good reserve","Same severity and same care"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease is the most common cause of secondary spontaneous pneumothorax. The symptoms are those of a primary episode but generally greater, because the underlying disease leaves less pulmonary reserve; the event can be life-threatening and needs more immediate intervention. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 19, 20, 24, 53, 56, 57"},{"q":"A 27-year-old man has a first primary spontaneous pneumothorax. On his upright chest radiograph, the apex-to-cupola distance is 4 cm and the interpleural distance at the level of the hilum is 1.5 cm. How is its size classified by the American College of Chest Physicians definition?","choices":["Large, by the apex distance","Small, by the hilum distance","Small, by the apex distance","Large, by the hilum distance"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The American College of Chest Physicians sizes a pneumothorax by the apex-to-cupola distance: under 3 cm is small, 3 cm or more is large. His 4 cm apex-to-cupola distance makes it large; the interpleural distance at the hilum is the British guideline measurement. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"A 27-year-old woman is being discharged after her primary spontaneous pneumothorax has resolved. She has a flight booked for a conference and asks for advice about air travel. Which advice is accurate?","choices":["Delay flying; confirm with a film","Fly once the chest pain is gone","Fly if her oxygen level is normal","Avoid air travel permanently"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Air travel is delayed for 7-14 days after resolution, and resolution is confirmed with a chest X-ray before she flies. Follow-up films verify complete lung re-expansion, and she is also counseled on recurrence and, if she smokes, on stopping. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 59, 60"},{"q":"A 35-year-old man is brought in after a motorcycle crash with decreased breath sounds on the right. His respiratory rate is 30 breaths per minute, heart rate 126 beats per minute and blood pressure 90/58 mm Hg, his trachea is midline and his neck veins are flat. The team needs a rapid answer at the bedside about a pneumothorax. Which test best fits?","choices":["Lateral decubitus film","Bedside lung ultrasound","Chest computed tomography","Arterial blood gas"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Lung ultrasound is becoming an indispensable bedside tool for the rapid diagnosis of pneumothorax and is beneficial for the unstable patient; absent lung sliding marks a pneumothorax. His tachycardia and hypotension make him unstable, so the test has to come to him. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 30, 33, 34, 35, 91"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman has a chylothorax after thoracic surgery, managed with a chest tube. Over several days the tube has drained large volumes of milky fluid, and she is losing weight. Which practice should be avoided?","choices":["Low long-chain fat diet","Intermittent thoracentesis","Excessive drainage","Octreotide therapy"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Chylothorax is managed with chest tube placement or intermittent thoracentesis while avoiding excessive drainage, because excessive loss of chyle can impact nutritional status. Her large volumes and weight loss show that risk. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 105, 106"},{"q":"A 26-year-old woman has a small primary spontaneous pneumothorax. Her respiratory rate is 20 breaths per minute, heart rate 96 beats per minute, blood pressure 118/74 mm Hg and oxygen saturation 96% on room air, and she speaks in whole sentences. How is she classified by the American College of Chest Physicians stability definition?","choices":["Unstable: respiratory rate","Unstable: heart rate","Unstable: oxygen saturation","Stable"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Every criterion is met: respiratory rate 20, under 24; heart rate 96, between 60 and 120; normal blood pressure; room-air saturation 96%, over 90%; and whole sentences. Stability requires all of these, and she has all of them. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"A 23-year-old woman has sudden fever and paroxysms of knife-like pleuritic pain lasting 15-30 minutes after a gastrointestinal illness, with tender intercostal muscles and a normal chest radiograph. Which laboratory finding supports the suspected diagnosis?","choices":["Purulent pleural fluid","Bloody pleural fluid","Elevated serum creatine kinase","Elevated pleural triglycerides"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. In pleurodynia, the virus, often coxsackie B, targets the striated intercostal muscles, causing inflammation or necrosis that frequently raises serum creatine kinase. Elevated creatine kinase is its listed diagnostic finding, and the chest radiograph is usually normal. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 98, 105, 108, 116, 118"},{"q":"A 46-year-old woman falls from a ladder and fractures several left ribs. The skin over her chest is intact, and a chest radiograph shows a visceral pleural line on the left. How is her pneumothorax classified?","choices":["Open traumatic","Iatrogenic","Primary spontaneous","Closed traumatic"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Blunt chest trauma in which a rib fracture tears the lung is a traumatic pneumothorax. With no external wound, air enters the pleural space from the lung because of the broken ribs, which makes it closed. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 17, 61, 62"},{"q":"A 38-year-old man has a new right pleural effusion. For 2 months he has had fevers, drenching night sweats, hemoptysis and a 6 kg weight loss. He has no leg swelling or orthopnea and no purulent sputum. Which is the most likely cause of his effusion?","choices":["Malignancy","Heart failure","Pneumonia","Tuberculosis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Fever, night sweats, hemoptysis and weight loss together are the extrapulmonary clue set that points to tuberculosis in a patient with an effusion. Tuberculosis is also the leading cause of exudative effusion worldwide. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 83, 86, 103"},{"q":"A 47-year-old man has sharp chest pain that worsens with deep breaths. On auscultation there is a grating, creaking friction rub over the lower anterior chest, and the clinician is unsure whether it arises from the pleura or the pericardium. Which bedside maneuver best separates the two?","choices":["Breath holding","Percussion for dullness","Palpation for fremitus","Having him cough"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A pleural friction rub disappears when the patient holds his breath, and that is the feature that differentiates it from a pericardial friction rub. The pleural rub comes from inflamed pleural layers rubbing together during respiration, so stopping respiration stops the sound. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 11, 12, 22, 87, 88"},{"q":"A 29-year-old woman with pleurisy has sharp pain at the base of the right chest and also pain at the tip of her right shoulder, although her shoulder examination is normal. She asks why her shoulder hurts. Which explanation is accurate?","choices":["A strained shoulder muscle","Phrenic nerve irritation","Intercostal nerve irritation","Visceral pleura pain fibers"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The parietal pleura has sensory innervation from intercostal and phrenic nerve fibers. When the inflamed parietal pleura lies on the central diaphragm, which is innervated by phrenic fibers, pain may be felt in the shoulder or neck on the same side, as hers is. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 3, 9, 11, 25"},{"q":"A 41-year-old woman on a ventilator after a fall suddenly becomes hypotensive at 74/46 mm Hg. Her trachea has shifted to the right, her neck veins are distended and breath sounds are absent on the left. No chest tube kit is readily available on the unit. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Chest computed tomography","Needle decompression","Chest radiograph first","Catheter aspiration"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Tension pneumothorax is a clinical diagnosis, and time is critical. Immediate chest tube placement is ideal, but when a chest tube is not readily available, immediate needle decompression is performed, followed by a moderate or large chest tube and admission. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 33, 39, 64, 66"},{"q":"A 26-year-old tall, thin man has acute dyspnea and sharp left-sided chest pain that worsens with deep breathing. His upright chest radiograph shows a thin, sharp visceral pleural line on the left with no lung markings beyond it. Which examination finding is expected on the left?","choices":["Increased tactile fremitus","Bronchial breath sounds","Hyperresonance to percussion","Dullness to percussion"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Percussion over a primary spontaneous pneumothorax is hyperresonant, because air fills the pleural space between the lung and the chest wall. His film confirms that air: a visceral pleural line with no bronchovascular markings beyond it. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 22, 23, 26, 87, 88"},{"q":"A 62-year-old woman with a new pleural effusion undergoes diagnostic thoracentesis. Pleural fluid protein is 3.9 g/dL with serum protein 6.5 g/dL (normal 6.0-8.3 g/dL). Pleural fluid lactate dehydrogenase is 150 units/L with serum lactate dehydrogenase 200 units/L (normal 140-280 units/L). How many of Light's criteria does the fluid meet, and what does that make it?","choices":["0 of 3; transudate","1 of 3; exudate","2 of 3; exudate","3 of 3; exudate"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Protein ratio 3.9 / 6.5 = 0.60, above 0.5: met. Lactate dehydrogenase ratio 150 / 200 = 0.75, above 0.6: met. Fluid lactate dehydrogenase 150 is under two-thirds of the 280 upper limit, about 187: not met. Two of three, and one is already enough for an exudate. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 101, 112"},{"q":"A 24-year-old tall, thin man has sudden dyspnea and sharp right-sided chest pain that began while he was sitting at his desk. He smokes cigarettes. He is tachypneic and tachycardic, with decreased chest excursion on the right, decreased tactile fremitus (vibrations from the larynx poorly transmitted to the chest wall) on the right, hyperresonance to percussion on the right and diminished breath sounds on the right. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Pleurisy","Pneumomediastinum","Spontaneous pneumothorax","Pleural effusion"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A tall, thin young man who smokes fits primary spontaneous pneumothorax, which often occurs at rest with acute dyspnea and ipsilateral pleuritic pain. Decreased excursion and fremitus, hyperresonance and diminished breath sounds on one side follow because pleural air blocks the transmission of vibration and sound. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 19, 21, 22, 23, 77, 87, 88"},{"q":"A 77-year-old man has a new right pleural effusion with no clear clinical cause, and diagnostic thoracentesis is performed. Pleural fluid protein is 1.8 g/dL with serum protein 6.9 g/dL (normal 6.0-8.3 g/dL). Pleural fluid lactate dehydrogenase is 80 units/L with serum lactate dehydrogenase 190 units/L (normal 140-280 units/L). How is the effusion classified by Light's criteria?","choices":["Exudate, by lactate dehydrogenase level","Transudate, no criterion met","Exudate, by protein ratio","Exudate, by lactate dehydrogenase ratio"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Protein ratio 1.8 / 6.9 = 0.26, not above 0.5. Lactate dehydrogenase ratio 80 / 190 = 0.42, not above 0.6. Fluid lactate dehydrogenase 80 is under two-thirds of the 280 upper limit, about 187. With all of Light's criteria absent, the fluid is a low-protein transudate. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 96, 101, 110, 112"},{"q":"A 24-year-old man has a primary spontaneous pneumothorax. His respiratory rate is 28 breaths per minute, heart rate 104 beats per minute, blood pressure 124/78 mm Hg and oxygen saturation 95% on room air, and he speaks in whole sentences. How is he classified by the American College of Chest Physicians stability definition?","choices":["Unstable: oxygen saturation","Unstable: respiratory rate","Stable","Unstable: heart rate"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Stability requires all of: respiratory rate under 24 breaths per minute, heart rate over 60 and under 120, normal blood pressure, room-air saturation over 90% and whole sentences. His heart rate of 104, normal pressure, saturation of 95% and full sentences pass, but a rate of 28 fails, and every other patient is unstable. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"An 18-year-old woman with spontaneous pneumomediastinum after an episode of violent coughing asks how coughing could put air in her chest. Which explanation is accurate?","choices":["Infection produced the gas","Alveoli ruptured under pressure","A chest wall wound let air in","An apical bleb burst"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Violent coughing is a sudden increase in intrathoracic pressure. Excessive intra-alveolar pressure leads to rupture of alveoli bordering the mediastinum, and the escaping air collects in the mediastinal tissues. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 18, 62, 75, 76"},{"q":"A 27-year-old man has simultaneous bilateral spontaneous pneumothoraces on his upright chest radiograph. He is stable while both sides are drained. Which definitive procedure is indicated?","choices":["Thoracoscopic surgery","Open thoracotomy","Bilateral catheter aspiration","Observation with oxygen"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Bilateral pneumothorax is an indication for video-assisted thoracoscopic surgery, an endoscopic procedure through small incisions that allows bleb stapling and pleurodesis. Catheter aspiration is contraindicated with bilateral pneumothoraces, and thoracoscopic surgery has supplanted open thoracotomy for pneumothorax. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 39, 47, 49, 50, 53"},{"q":"A 47-year-old man had a traumatic hemothorax that was only partly drained, and a collection of blood remains in his pleural space weeks later. Which complication does this retained blood most directly risk?","choices":["Chylothorax","Pneumomediastinum","Empyema","Tension pneumothorax"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Undrained blood collections serve as a primary nidus for infection, and a retained hemothorax leads directly to empyema, as well as fibrothorax and trapped lung. That is why tube thoracostomy, the essential first step, aims to evacuate the blood. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 63, 74, 75, 104"},{"q":"A 30-year-old woman is scheduled for pleurodesis after her third spontaneous pneumothorax. She asks what the procedure will do inside her chest. Which explanation is accurate?","choices":["Makes the pleural layers adhere","Staples ruptured blebs shut","Drains air through a valve","Removes the visceral pleura"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Pleurodesis obliterates the pleural space by inducing inflammation there: an irritant causes adhesions of the two layers of pleura, which helps keep the lung inflated after pneumothorax and leaves no room for fluid in an effusion. It is used to prevent recurrent or treat resistant pneumothorax. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 40, 45, 46, 47"},{"q":"A 29-year-old commercial airline pilot has recovered from his first primary spontaneous pneumothorax, and a follow-up film confirms that the lung has re-expanded. He does not smoke and has no family history of pneumothorax. Which referral is recommended?","choices":["Early surgical evaluation","Genetics referral","Smoking cessation program","No referral; resume flying"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Pilots and divers need early surgical options for safety, and video-assisted thoracoscopic surgery closes leaks to prevent future collapses. Recurrence approaches 50% within three years, a risk his occupation cannot carry. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 59, 60"},{"q":"A 34-year-old woman with systemic lupus erythematosus has 2 days of sharp, well-localized pain over the left chest wall that worsens with deep breathing, coughing and sneezing. She is tachypneic, with limited chest wall expansion on the left. A pleural friction rub (a grating, creaking sound) is heard in both phases of respiration and disappears when she holds her breath. Her chest radiograph is normal. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Costochondritis","Pneumothorax","Pleurisy","Pericarditis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Pleurisy is inflammation of the parietal pleura, and inflammatory disorders such as systemic lupus erythematosus are among its causes. Well-localized sharp pain made worse by breathing, coughing and sneezing, limited expansion on the affected side and a pleural friction rub fit, and a rub that disappears with breath holding comes from the pleura. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 9, 10, 11, 12, 13, 23, 26"},{"q":"A 52-year-old woman with a rib fracture from a fall becomes acutely hypotensive, with tracheal deviation to the right, distended neck veins and absent breath sounds on the left. A chest tube kit is at the bedside. Which action should be avoided?","choices":["Hospital admission","Chest tube at the midaxillary line","Waiting for a chest radiograph","Immediate chest tube placement"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Tension pneumothorax is a medical emergency and a clinical diagnosis: time is critical, and there is no time for the X-ray. With a chest tube readily available, immediate chest tube placement is ideal, so waiting for imaging only wastes the time she does not have. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 41, 65, 66, 68"},{"q":"A 78-year-old woman with known heart failure has increasing lower extremity edema, orthopnea and paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnea, and her chest radiograph shows bilateral pleural effusions. She is comfortable at rest. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Diagnostic thoracentesis","Chemical pleurodesis","Indwelling pleural catheter","Diuretic therapy"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Her edema, orthopnea and paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnea give the effusions a clear clinical cause: heart failure, which raises capillary hydrostatic pressure and causes more than 90% of transudates. Effusions are managed by treating the underlying disorder, and diuretics are the initial treatment for heart failure transudates. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 82, 86, 96, 97, 111, 113"},{"q":"A 31-year-old man undergoes catheter aspiration of a primary spontaneous pneumothorax through the second intercostal space at the midclavicular line. More than 2 liters of air have been withdrawn through the stopcock, and air is still coming back. What should the clinician suspect?","choices":["Tension pneumothorax","Full lung re-expansion","A new hemothorax","A persistent air leak"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. During catheter aspiration, removal of more than 2 liters should raise concern for a persistent air leak: air keeps entering the pleural space as it is withdrawn. A post-procedure chest radiograph follows, and failure of aspiration is an indication for a chest tube. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 38, 54, 64, 69"},{"q":"A 50-year-old woman in the intensive care unit becomes acutely short of breath after an attempted subclavian vein catheter placement, and bedside lung ultrasound is performed over the left chest. Which ultrasound finding indicates a pneumothorax?","choices":["Lung sliding present","B-lines present","Seashore pattern","Absent lung sliding"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. On lung ultrasound, lung sliding is present in the normal lung and absent with a pneumothorax, the primary finding that the pleural layers are no longer moving against each other. On M-mode, the stratosphere sign, parallel horizontal lines, replaces the normal seashore pattern. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 30, 34"},{"q":"A 67-year-old man has progressive dyspnea and a dry cough. Over the lower right chest there is dullness to percussion, decreased tactile fremitus (vibrations from the larynx poorly felt on the chest wall) and diminished breath sounds, with bronchial breath sounds at the upper edge of the dull area. Which condition best explains these findings?","choices":["Pleural effusion","Pneumothorax","Pneumonia infiltrate","Pleurisy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Dyspnea and dry cough are the most common symptoms of pleural effusion. Fluid is dull to percussion and obstructs the transmission of vibrations, so fremitus decreases; breath sounds are diminished over the effusion, while bronchial breath sounds increase toward its top, exactly his examination. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 12, 23, 85, 87, 88, 89"}]};
